  <section class="smg-section smg-meet" id="meet-connoisseur-joe-dampt">
    <div class="smg-meet__media">
      <figure><LicensedImage src={joePhoto} alt="Joe Dampt playing a Gibson guitar" width={720} loading="lazy" /></figure>
      <div class="smg-meet__video"><lite-youtube videoid="fTpIquyV-j8" playlabel="Play Meet Joe Dampt of Joe's Vintage Guitars"></lite-youtube></div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="smg-eyebrow">Meet the Buyer</p>
      <h2>I'm Joe Dampt</h2>
      <p>I buy vintage Gibson electrics, acoustics, archtops and basses from owners in all 50 states. My offers are higher than a typical trade-in or local music-store offer, and every appraisal and cash offer is free with no obligation. When an in-person meeting makes sense, I can inspect the guitar and buy it on the spot.</p>
      <p>I've spent more than twelve years buying vintage guitars full-time and have appraised more than 10,000 instruments. I own Joe's Vintage Guitars in Mesa, Arizona, where I meet sellers by appointment. I can meet many owners in the Phoenix area, and sellers elsewhere can use my prepaid FedEx process.</p>
      <p>I pay attention to the things that separate one Gibson from another: the finish, pickups, electronics, hardware, repairs and the story that came with it. If I can buy your guitar, I'll explain what I see and make you an offer.</p>
      <div class="smg-credential"><strong>Published by Vintage Verified</strong><p>Vintage Verified is an independent Nashville lab that analyzes the finishes, plastics and hardware on vintage guitars and doesn't buy, sell or value them. I wrote a guest essay for them on authentication and have no affiliation with the lab.</p><a href="https://www.vintageverified.com/joes-vintage" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read My Vintage Verified Essay</a></div>
      <div class="smg-as-seen"><strong>As Seen In</strong><a href="https://www.kjzz.org/the-show/see-inside-joes-vintage-guitars-in-mesa" target="_blank" rel="noopener">KJZZ 91.5</a><a href="https://voyagephoenix.com/interview/exploring-life-business-with-joseph-dampt-of-joes-vintage-guitars-llc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">VoyagePhoenix</a><a href="https://www.vintageverified.com/joes-vintage" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vintage Verified</a><a href="/reverb-reviews/">Reverb Top-Rated Seller</a></div>
      <a class="smg-text-link" href="/about-me/">Learn More About Joe</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="smg-section smg-preservation">
    <figure><LicensedImage src={byrdlandPhoto} alt="Original owner Don Majure with his sunburst 1957 Gibson Byrdland" width={820} loading="lazy" /></figure>
    <div>
      <p class="smg-eyebrow">Provenance Matters</p>
      <h2>Original Photos And Stories Can Add To My Offer</h2>
      <p>When a Gibson comes with original-owner photographs, receipts or a well-documented family story, I put real value on that history. Meaningful provenance can increase what I'm willing to pay because the people and stories behind vintage guitars matter to me.</p>
      <p>I keep those materials with the guitar. I often print the owner's story and place it inside the case so the next owner knows who played it before them.</p>
      <p>If you inherited a Gibson, don't worry if you don't know its model or year. Keep the case, old parts, receipts and photographs together, then send me everything you have. I'll consider those details when making my offer.</p>
      <a class="smg-text-link" href="/post/one-owner-1957-gibson-byrdland/">Read the One-Owner Byrdland Story</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="smg-section smg-expertise">
    <div><p class="smg-eyebrow">For Sellers Who Want To Learn More</p><h2>Learn More About Your Gibson Before You Sell</h2></div>
    <div>
      <p>You don't have to research your guitar before contacting me. That's what I'm here to help with. Send me what you know, and I'll work through the details myself.</p>
      <p>If you'd like to learn more before deciding whether to sell, these Gibson resources explain how serial numbers, parts, production totals and model years fit together. I enjoy working with sellers who've done some research because it makes it easier to talk through the guitar, explain my offer and make sure we're on the same page.</p>
      <ul class="smg-resource-links">
        <li><a href="/how-to-read-gibson-serial-numbers/">Date your guitar with my Gibson serial-number guide</a></li>
        <li><a href="/post/what-a-serial-number-cant-tell-you/">Learn what a Gibson serial number can and can't tell you</a></li>
        <li><a href="/post/gibson-paf-patent-number-pickup-guide/">Compare PAF and Patent Number pickups</a></li>
        <li><a href="/post/gibson-shipping-totals-1948-1979/">Review Gibson production and shipping totals</a></li>
        <li><a href="/post/how-the-year-of-manufacture-of-your-vintage-gibson-guitar-affects-its-price/">See how the year can affect vintage Gibson value</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="smg-section smg-options" id="best-way-to-sell">
    <header class="smg-heading"><p class="smg-eyebrow">Know Your Options</p><h2>The Best Way to Sell a Vintage Gibson: Dealer, Consignment, Auction or Reverb?</h2><p>Selling directly to me gets you a cash offer with no listing, fees or wait. Consignment or auction can bring more if the guitar sells, but you pay a commission and wait for a buyer. If you'd rather list it yourself, Reverb is where I'd point you. Just factor in the fees.</p></header>
    <table class="smg-options__table">
      <caption>Ways to sell a vintage Gibson</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Option</th><th scope="col">Why People Choose It</th><th scope="col">What to Consider</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Sell Directly to Me</th><td data-label="Why People Choose It">A cash offer, no public listing, no seller fees and help with the handoff.</td><td data-label="What to Consider">I pay among the highest dealer prices, but my offer will still be a little below the guitar's full retail market value.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Consignment</th><td data-label="Why People Choose It">You may receive more if the guitar sells at the asking price.</td><td data-label="What to Consider">You pay a commission and may wait weeks or months for a buyer.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Auction</th><td data-label="Why People Choose It">Several bidders can compete for a rare or well-known guitar.</td><td data-label="What to Consider">The final price is uncertain, and seller fees, photography and shipping still matter.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Reverb or eBay Listing</th><td data-label="Why People Choose It">You control the asking price and speak directly with buyers. Reverb also offers strong shipping protection.</td><td data-label="What to Consider">Factor in the fees and remember that disputes can favor the buyer. I wouldn't choose this for a first-time sale over $4,000.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Music-Store Trade-In</th><td data-label="Why People Choose It">You can finish the deal in a local store without shipping the guitar.</td><td data-label="What to Consider">Trade-in offers are typically well under market because the store needs room for resale costs and profit.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="smg-options__insurance">If you compare dealers, ask how the guitar will be insured during the handoff. Most established dealers carry more than $100,000 in insurance.</p>
  </section>

  <span class="smg-anchor" id="receive-the-highest-payout"></span>
  <section class="smg-section smg-guides" id="guitar-families">
    <header class="smg-heading"><p class="smg-eyebrow">Gibson Models I Buy</p><h2>Vintage Gibson Guitars I Buy</h2><p>I buy vintage Gibson solid-body, thinline, acoustic and archtop guitars from owners across the country. The cards below show the eras and models I'm looking for. If you don't see your Gibson listed, send it anyway. I also buy unusual models, mandolins and guitars with old repairs or changed parts.</p><a class="smg-text-link" href="#appraisal">Tell Me About Your Gibson</a></header>
